We prove the first part of the lemma directly.
Upon choosing ${q}_0 = \frac {1} {d+1}$, we have from \eqref{eq:convert_delta} that 
\begin{align*}
    {\Delta}({q}_0) &= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{d-1}{j} {q}_0^j (1-{q}_0)^{d-j} (f(j+1) - f(j)) \\ 
    &\ge {q}_0^0 (1-{q}_0)^{d}  (f(1) - f(0)) \\
    &\ge \frac{f(1) - f(0)}{e}, 
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from the fact that $\left(1-\frac{1}{d+1}\right)^d$ is decreasing in $d$ and $\lim_{d\to \infty}\left(1-\frac{1}{d+1}\right)^d=\frac 1 e$.
Then, plugging this into \eqref{eq:def:fTp}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    {\hat{\Gamma}} ({q}_0) = \frac{36.06(1-{q}_0)}{{q}_0 ({\Delta}({q}_0))^2} \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon} \right) \le \frac{266.45}{(f(1) - f(0))^2} d \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon} \right) ,
\end{align*}
which yields \eqref{eq:existence_margin_z} as desired. 


We now turn to prove the second part of the lemma. Under the choice ${q}_0 = \frac {d} {d+1}$, we have from \eqref{eq:convert_delta} that
\begin{align*}
